CAT = "abgLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("Which section of the Charter gives everyone the right not to be arbitrarily detained or imprisoned?",
 "Section 9", ["Section 7", "Section 8", "Section 10"],
 "Section 9 protects against arbitrary detention or imprisonment. Section 8 covers unreasonable search or seizure and section 10 the rights on arrest."),
("Section 8 of the Charter protects everyone against:",
 "Unreasonable search", ["Arbitrary detention", "Cruel punishment", "Testifying against themselves"],
 "Section 8 gives everyone the right to be secure against unreasonable search or seizure. Arbitrary detention is section 9."),
("Under section 10 of the Charter, a person who is arrested has the right to:",
 "Get a lawyer without delay", ["Leave after one hour", "Have a friend present", "Refuse to give their name"],
 "Section 10 gives the right to be told the reasons promptly, to retain and instruct counsel without delay and to be informed of that right, and to habeas corpus."),
("A licensed guard's power to arrest someone comes from:",
 "The powers any citizen has", ["The powers of a peace officer", "The client's site policy", "The guard's licence card"],
 "A security licence gives no police powers. A guard arrests under section 494 of the Criminal Code, which applies to anyone."),
("Under section 494(1)(a), anyone may arrest without warrant a person found committing:",
 "An indictable offence", ["A provincial offence", "A municipal bylaw offence", "Any traffic offence"],
 "Section 494(1)(a) lets any person arrest someone they find committing an indictable offence. Hybrid offences count as indictable for this purpose."),
("To 'find someone committing' an offence for a citizen's arrest means you:",
 "Saw the act itself", ["Saw only the damage", "Heard about it later", "Suspect it from clues"],
 "Being found committing means catching the person in the act. Seeing the start or the aftermath, or hearing about it, is not enough."),
("A store's loss prevention worker chases a man shouting that he is under arrest. You may help arrest him if you believe on reasonable grounds that he:",
 "Committed a crime", ["Owes the store money", "Broke a store rule", "Looks like a suspect"],
 "Section 494(1)(b) allows an arrest of a person you believe on reasonable grounds has committed a criminal offence and is escaping from, and freshly pursued by, persons with lawful authority to arrest."),
("You guard a mall for its owner and see a person commit a summary conviction offence against mall property. You:",
 "May arrest the person", ["Have no arrest power", "Need a warrant first", "Must wait for two days"],
 "Section 494(2) lets the owner or a person authorized by the owner arrest anyone found committing any criminal offence on or in relation to the property, not only indictable ones."),
("You saw a theft at your store on Monday. The thief returns on Wednesday. Under s 494(2)(b), arresting now is lawful only if it is within a reasonable time and:",
 "Police can't feasibly do it", ["Your manager approves", "The item is still missing", "The thief admits the theft"],
 "Section 494(2)(b) allows an arrest within a reasonable time after the offence if you believe on reasonable grounds it is not feasible for a peace officer to make it. Call police and report Monday's theft."),
("After making a citizen's arrest, the Criminal Code requires you to:",
 "Hand them to police forthwith", ["Hold them until shift change", "Release them after a warning", "Take them to the client"],
 "Section 494(3) says anyone other than a peace officer who arrests without warrant shall forthwith deliver the person to a peace officer."),
("A shopper tells you they saw someone hide a watch in a coat. You did not see it. You should:",
 "Watch them and call police", ["Arrest them on that word", "Search their coat pockets", "Hold them until they confess"],
 "You did not find the person committing the offence, so section 494 gives you no arrest power. Observe, take notes and let police act."),
("Under section 322(2) of the Criminal Code, theft is complete when a person intending to steal first:",
 "Moves the item", ["Leaves the store", "Passes the tills", "Sells the item"],
 "Section 322(2) says theft is committed when, with intent to steal, the person moves the thing or causes it to be moved."),
("Even though theft is complete once an item is moved, the course recommends loss prevention arrests only after the person:",
 "Passes the tills and leaves", ["Picks up the item", "Puts the item in a bag", "Walks past one aisle"],
 "A person stopped inside the store can say they meant to pay. Waiting until they pass all tills and leave removes that doubt and makes an unlawful arrest less likely."),
("A guard checks bags at a concert gate. Because a guard has no legal power to search, this is:",
 "A consent search", ["A Charter search", "A warrant search", "A lawful seizure"],
 "A guard has no power in law to search people or their property. A gate bag check is a condition of entry the person can accept or refuse."),
("A fan refuses to let you check their bag at a venue that requires it. You may:",
 "Refuse them entry", ["Search the bag anyway", "Arrest them for it", "Take the bag from them"],
 "The person keeps the right to refuse the search, and you keep the right to deny entry. Searching or seizing without consent is not lawful."),
("Halfway through a consent bag check, the person says to stop. You should:",
 "Stop at once", ["Finish quickly", "Detain them", "Keep the bag"],
 "Consent can be withdrawn at any time. When the person says stop, the search must end immediately, and you may then deny entry."),
("A venue bans outside alcohol. A fan with a bottle refuses to give it up. You may:",
 "Deny entry, not seize it", ["Seize it, then admit them", "Pour it out, then admit them", "Arrest them for having it"],
 "A guard has no authority under the Gaming, Liquor and Cannabis Act to seize liquor. Explain the choice: dispose of it or do not enter."),
("Under the Trespass to Premises Act, notice not to trespass may be given:",
 "Orally, in writing or signs", ["Only by posted signs", "Only in a written letter", "Only through the police"],
 "Section 2(2) allows notice orally or in writing by the owner or an authorized representative, or by signs visibly displayed."),
("For signs to give notice under the Trespass to Premises Act, they must be displayed at:",
 "Each normal entrance", ["The main entrance only", "Every window on site", "The nearest street corner"],
 "Section 2(2)(b) requires signs visibly displayed at each entrance normally used to enter the premises. A person is then deemed to have had notice."),
("Under the Trespass to Premises Act, 'premises' includes:",
 "Its parking area", ["Only the building itself", "Only fenced farmland", "Only public sidewalks"],
 "Section 1(c) defines premises to include land used with a building or structure, such as parking, display or storage areas."),
("A guard acting for the owner apprehends a trespasser under the Trespass to Premises Act. The guard must then:",
 "Hand them to a peace officer", ["Issue them a written fine", "Photograph and release them", "Escort them to the gate"],
 "Section 5 lets the owner or an authorized representative apprehend a trespasser, and anyone other than a peace officer must deliver the trespasser to a peace officer as soon as practicable."),
("A trespass is committed by driving a car onto posted premises. Under the Trespass to Premises Act, who is guilty?",
 "The driver", ["The car's owner", "Each passenger", "The insurer"],
 "Section 4 makes the driver of the vehicle guilty of the contravention when a trespass is committed by means of a motor vehicle."),
("A person got permission to enter a site by lying about who they were. Under the Trespass to Premises Act they are:",
 "Deemed a trespasser", ["Lawfully on the site", "Guilty only of fraud", "Allowed one warning"],
 "Section 2(4) says a person who obtains permission to enter by false pretences is deemed to have trespassed."),
("Under the Petty Trespass Act, entry is prohibited without any notice on land that is:",
 "Fenced or enclosed", ["Open and unmarked", "Next to a highway", "Owned by a town or city"],
 "Section 2.2 prohibits entry without notice on lawns, gardens, land used for crops, animals or bees, and land surrounded by a fence or natural boundary or otherwise enclosed."),
("A person told by the occupier's guard to leave land commits an offence under the Petty Trespass Act if they:",
 "Don't leave immediately", ["Ask why they must go", "Leave by a side gate", "Leave within a minute"],
 "Section 2(1)(b) makes it an offence to fail to leave land immediately after being directed to by the owner, occupier or a person they authorize."),
("A courier walks up the path to a building's front door to deliver a parcel. Under the Petty Trespass Act this is:",
 "Presumed lawful", ["Always a trespass", "A trespass after dark", "A criminal offence"],
 "Section 2(4) presumes that access for lawful purposes to a building's door by a pathway provided for that purpose is not a trespass."),
("Under the Petty Trespass Act, tearing down a no-trespassing sign posted by the occupier is:",
 "An offence", ["Allowed for tenants", "A civil matter only", "Legal if faded"],
 "Section 2.1(4) bars tearing down, removing, damaging, defacing or covering up a sign that prohibits trespass, and section 2.1(5) makes it an offence."),
("Taking an item 'without colour of right', as in the definition of theft, means taking it without:",
 "An honest claim", ["A receipt for it", "Anyone watching", "Using any force"],
 "Theft under section 322 requires a fraudulent taking without colour of right, meaning with no honest belief that one is entitled to the item."),
("Which act counts as 'breaking' for break and enter?",
 "Picking a door's lock", ["Walking in an open door", "Reading the door sign", "Knocking on the door"],
 "Breaking includes opening anything used to cover an opening, such as picking the lock on a secured office. Section 348 then requires entry with intent to commit an indictable offence."),
("Someone wilfully deletes a company's computer records to stop staff using them. Under section 430 this is:",
 "Mischief", ["Theft", "Fraud", "Trespassing"],
 "Section 430(1.1) makes it mischief to wilfully destroy or alter computer data or render it useless, or to interfere with its lawful use."),
("A man raises his fist and threatens to hit you while standing close enough to do it. Under section 265 this is:",
 "An assault", ["Not yet an offence", "Only rude conduct", "Only mischief"],
 "Section 265(1)(b) says threatening by an act or gesture to apply force, with the present ability to do so, is an assault even before any blow lands."),
("For the offence of possessing property obtained by crime, the person must:",
 "Know it came from crime", ["Have stolen it personally", "Have held it a week", "Have tried to sell it"],
 "Section 354 makes it an offence to possess property knowing it was obtained by an indictable offence. The person need not have stolen it."),
]
